// Reassigning an `owned` `var` that was never moved freed nothing: the old
// value leaked on every `v = ...`. Not an R11 "Still broken" row (R11 is
// about `arc`); OWNERSHIP.md's R16 paragraph carried it together with the
// revival leak, and `emitAssign`'s pre-drop was `arc`-only because a list
// literal `[s]` once copied the header without marking `s` moved, so freeing
// the old value would have freed under that element. borrowck refuses that
// list element since c314a0e, and no other untracked alias of an `owned`
// String or list is accepted.
//
// Each iteration reassigns a String var and a list var in `main`, and one
// var in each of the two later-move helpers, so the count before any fix
// was 4000 on both witnesses.
//
// Also covered since the same day: a var moved AFTER its reassignment
// (`later_return`, `later_sink`). The pre-drop is decided per store from
// borrowck's `assign_liveness`, not from "moved anywhere in the function",
// so those stores release the old value too; before that they measured one
// leak per call each.
//
// NOT covered, by design: a store whose target was already moved (R3a
// revival, a one-branch move, `v = pass(v)`), and any store inside a
// `while` body that also moves the target, because the back edge can carry
// that move to the store. Those keep the leak, and a revived var's final
// value still leaks at scope end (`wasMoved` stays permanent for the
// scope-end drop).
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it; lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus, agreement and
// mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn sink(owned s: String) {
}

pub fn later_return() -> String {
    var owned v: String = "later-a"
    v = "later-b"
    return v
}

pub fn later_sink() {
    var owned v: String = "sunk-a"
    v = "sunk-b"
    sink(v)
}

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        var owned s: String = "first-string"
        s = "second-string"
        var owned xs: [Int] = [1, 2, 3]
        xs = [4, 5]
        let owned r: String = later_return()
        later_sink()
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
